Q19. What is the difference between global placement and detailed placement?

Global placement distributes cells and optimizes placement objectives, sometimes allowing temporary overlaps. Detailed placement legalizes the cells and aligns them to valid placement sites and rows.


Q20. What is a placement blockage?

A placement blockage restricts or prevents standard-cell placement in a specified region. It can protect macro areas, preserve routing space, or reserve space for other design requirements.


Q21. What is CTS?

Clock Tree Synthesis builds the clock distribution network from the clock source to sequential elements. It inserts clock buffers or inverters to control clock skew, latency, transition time, and clock-tree power.


Q24. What is the difference between Classic CTS and CCD?

Classic CTS builds the clock tree according to the specified clock constraints and optimization objectives. Concurrent Clock and Data optimization considers clock and data paths together and may adjust clock timing, including useful skew, to improve overall timing. The exact implementation depends on the tool version and flow settings.


Q25. What is the difference between setup and hold effects after CTS?

Changes in clock arrival times can improve setup timing but worsen hold timing, or vice versa. Therefore, both setup and hold checks must be performed after CTS.


Q26. What is routing?

Routing connects cells and macros according to the netlist. Global routing estimates routing paths and resource usage, while detailed routing creates the actual metal and via connections while satisfying routing rules.


Q27. What is DRC?

Design Rule Checking verifies whether the layout follows manufacturing rules, including spacing, width, enclosure, and via-related requirements.


Q28. You mentioned fixing post-route DRC violations. How did you approach them?

“I reviewed the DRC reports to identify the violating locations and nets. I then investigated the routing geometry and used appropriate rerouting or physical optimization before rerunning DRC to verify the corrections.”


Q29. What is LVS?

Layout Versus Schematic compares the connectivity extracted from the physical layout against the reference netlist. It checks whether the implemented layout has matching connectivity.


Q30. What is post-route optimization?

Post-route optimization improves timing and other design objectives using actual or estimated routed interconnect parasitics. It may involve cell resizing, buffering, and routing changes.


Q34. How do you fix setup violations?

I inspect the critical timing path and identify whether the delay comes from cells, interconnect, fanout, or clock timing. Depending on the cause, I may optimize cell sizing, placement, buffering, or routing, then rerun STA.

Q35. How do you fix hold violations?

I identify the hold-violating paths and add suitable data-path delay, commonly through delay buffers or other tool-driven optimization. I then rerun hold and setup analysis to ensure both checks pass.


Q37. What is a timing exception?

A timing exception changes how the timing tool analyzes a particular path. Examples include false-path and multicycle-path constraints. Exceptions must reflect the real design behavior and should not be used simply to hide timing violations.


